From 1b6c6a06056529da2c85a191038ef84faede72ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aaron Wohl <wohl@awohl.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 07:39:09 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Typed store primitives respect the write barrier

The primitives that store a typed value into the bytes of an object
(primitiveStore*IntoBytes) and primitiveFFIIntegerAtPut did not test the
receiver for immutability.  A store into a read-only ByteArray changed
the bytes, and the primitive did not fail:

    g := ByteArray new: 8.  g beReadOnlyObject.
    g float64AtOffset: 0 put: 2.5.   "g is now #[0 0 0 0 0 0 4 64]"
    g uint32AtOffset: 0 put: 7.      "g is now #[7 0 0 0 ...]"

Each C primitive now fails with PrimErrNoModification for a read-only
receiver, as primitiveAtPut does.  The image fallback code then decides
which error the caller sees.  primitiveFFIIntegerAtPut does the test
only for a store into the bytes of the receiver itself; a store through
an ExternalAddress goes to outside memory.

The JIT has machine code versions of these primitives
(genPrimitiveStore:intoBytesWith:needsToTrap:).  The machine code now
leaves without a store when the receiver is read-only, so the C
primitive or the fallback code of the method handles the case.  Without
this part the machine code had no test for a read-only receiver.

Tests: VMPrimitiveTest checks Float64, Int32 and UInt8 stores into a
read-only and a mutable ByteArray.  VMByteArrayAccessPrimitive checks
primitiveFFIIntegerAtPut for a read-only ByteArray, a mutable
ByteArray and an ExternalAddress.  VMJittedByteArrayAccessPrimitiveTest
checks that the machine code of Int32, UInt64 and Float64 stores leaves
a read-only receiver unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
